ic cough producing mucus since birth and frontal sinus symptoms argue for cystic fibrosis, given the high danger score and lack of growth failure despite typical malabsorption in CF?", "summary": "The chronic respiratory symptoms and airway dilation on MRI support a structural lung disease, but the absence of failure to thrive weakens cystic fibrosis as a primary diagnosis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the MRI showing airway dilation and chronic mucus production since birth support a diagnosis of chronic pulmonary infection with structural lung disease?", "summary": "MRI findings of airway dilation and chronic mucus production strongly indicate structural lung disease, aligning with chronic pulmonary infection."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of tricuspid stenosis and recurrent respiratory infections argue for congenital heart disease as the primary pulmonary involvement mechanism?", "summary": "Tricuspid stenosis may explain pulmonary symptoms but does not strongly link to structural lung disease or cystic fibrosis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the persistent 25th percentile growth since birth without documented failure to thrive argue against cystic fibrosis as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Normal growth despite chronic symptoms moderately reduces the likelihood of cystic fibrosis but does not rule it out."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the documented history of tricuspid stenosis instead of typical bronchiectasis features (like clubbing or hemoptysis) suggest a cardiac rather than structural pulmonary cause?", "summary": "The presence of tricuspid stenosis weakly suggests a cardiac etiology for pulmonary symptoms, but structural lung disease remains more likely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of skeletal system abnormalities or nail aplasia in the physical exam challenge congenital heart disease as